---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';

const ways = [
  ['nohup / disown', 'Keeps a command running after logout', 'No interactive session to come back to; output only in a file'],
  ['tmux or screen on the server', 'Interactive sessions that survive disconnects', 'Install it per host, ssh in and reattach by hand, prefix keys, its own scrollback'],
  ['mosh', 'A connection that survives roaming and sleep', 'No reattaching: once the client is closed, you can’t get back to that session'],
  ['Swarm', 'Native tabs for sessions on the server that reconnect by themselves', 'The app is macOS only'],
];
---

<Guide
  kind="# guide"
  title="Persistent SSH sessions on macOS that survive sleep and disconnects | Swarm"
  description="How to keep SSH sessions and long-running jobs alive on a Mac through sleep, Wi-Fi changes and dropped connections: nohup, tmux, mosh, and Swarm's persistent remote sessions."
>
  <h1>Persistent SSH sessions on macOS</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Close your MacBook during a long build on a server and the SSH connection drops; the shell on the server gets a
    hang-up and the build dies with it. Here is how to keep remote sessions alive through sleep, network changes and
    disconnects.
  </p>

  <h2>The options</h2>
  <div class="table">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>The catch</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ways.map(([how, good, catchText]) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{how}</strong></td><td>{good}</td><td>{catchText}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>With Swarm</h2>
  <p>
    Swarm is a macOS terminal whose tabs are persistent sessions. For a server, it runs a small daemon,{' '}
    <code>swarmd</code>, on the host; the daemon owns your shells there, so they don't depend on the SSH connection.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Connect.</strong> Click <strong>Connect…</strong> in the sidebar and pick a host from <code>~/.ssh/config</code>, or type <code>user@host[:port]</code>. Swarm uses your keys, agent and jump hosts, and never asks for a password itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Let it install the daemon.</strong> If the host doesn't have <code>swarmd</code>, Swarm offers to install it in your home folder. No root is needed, and Linux and macOS hosts both work.</li>
    <li><strong>Work as usual.</strong> Open tabs on the host, run builds, tail logs, start servers. Sessions for each host sit in their own group in the sidebar.</li>
    <li><strong>Close the lid.</strong> When your Mac wakes or joins another network, the tabs show that they're reconnecting and pick up where they were, with the output that arrived meanwhile.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    On Linux hosts, turn on lingering (Settings → Hosts, or <code>loginctl enable-linger</code>) so that systemd keeps
    your sessions when your last login there ends.
  </p>

  <h2>From the command line</h2>
  <p>The same remote sessions are available from any terminal with <code>swarmctl</code>:</p>
  <pre class="term"><span class="p">$</span> swarmctl ls --host build-box
<span class="p">$</span> swarmctl new -d -n nightly --host build-box   <span class="d"># start a detached session</span>
<span class="p">$</span> swarmctl attach nightly --host build-box      <span class="d"># ctrl-\ detaches</span></pre>

  <h2>Setting up servers in advance</h2>
  <p>
    To prepare hosts with a script or configuration management, unpack the static Linux build of <code>swarmd</code>{' '}
    into <code>~/.local/share/swarm/bin</code>; the app finds it there and keeps it updated. See{' '}
    <a href="/docs/#server-install">install on a server yourself</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
